00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:31.580
And then someone would
actually go ahead and do that.

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:33.079
RUSSELL SMITH: Yeah,
the classic one

00:06:33.079 --> 00:06:35.530
would be to ask them to sign
up and ask them to check

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:38.050
if the e-mail was received
or if they got a text

00:06:38.050 --> 00:06:39.130
message to a number.

00:06:39.130 --> 00:06:43.075
And all those kind of extra
tooling around e-mail checking,

00:06:43.075 --> 00:06:47.500
third party auth, getting
phone numbers and stuff

00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:51.194
is all handled by us and
automatic and pretty awesome.

00:06:51.194 --> 00:06:52.110
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:56.730
So just to have an idea,
how does it actually work?

00:06:56.730 --> 00:06:59.697
Like if I do this thing-- I was
like, I have my version, right?

00:06:59.697 --> 00:07:01.030
RUSSELL SMITH: Classic question.

00:07:01.030 --> 00:07:03.860
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Like, how does
it-- like I have my version.

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:06.520
And I'm like, oh yeah, I
want to run some tests.

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:08.490
So I can give you
my script, which

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:12.222
is like, for instance, I'll
know, log into gcpodcast.com

00:07:12.222 --> 00:07:14.800
and click on Download and
visit 45, for instance.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:16.580
RUSSELL SMITH: That
would be a great test.

00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:17.620
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:20.790
RUSSELL SMITH: So OK, basically
we store all the stuff for you,

00:07:20.790 --> 00:07:22.810
all your tests for you,
or you can store them

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:25.360
in plain text in your repo and
then just upload them to us

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:26.190
automatically.

00:07:26.190 --> 00:07:28.070
Once you actually
hit our API and say,

00:07:28.070 --> 00:07:31.220
hey, Rainforest,
run all these tests,

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:32.276
we do a couple of things.

00:07:32.276 --> 00:07:34.150
One, we copy all those
tests and version them

00:07:34.150 --> 00:07:36.275
so that if you ever change
your test in the future,

00:07:36.275 --> 00:07:37.600
we know which ones we ran.

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:39.750
But then the next thing
is we start farming it out

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:41.360
to the workers we've got online.

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:43.870
So normally we have a
few thousand workers,

00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:46.329
up to like 5000 workers
online at any one time.

00:07:46.329 --> 00:07:47.870
And we're talking
about this earlier,

00:07:47.870 --> 00:07:51.950
but we basically give out the
work in kind of a special way.

00:07:51.950 --> 00:07:55.710
So we use Elixir projects.

00:07:55.710 --> 00:07:58.110
This is one of our first
Elixir production apps

00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:00.360
that connects to
a Chrome extension

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:01.870
that the testers download.

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:04.760
So we know which testers are
online when and when they

00:08:04.760 --> 00:08:05.330
want to work.

00:08:05.330 --> 00:08:07.570
So much like, say,
Uber, when you

00:08:07.570 --> 00:08:09.550
want to do rides
as an Uber driver,

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:12.860
you just open up the app and
say hey, I want to do rides.

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:15.420
For our testers, they
just open up their browser

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:18.400
and then they just click
on the icon for Rainforest.

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:19.580
And it just turns it on.

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:21.470
And we know that they want work.

00:08:21.470 --> 00:08:23.750
And then we can just
pipe the work to them.

00:08:23.750 --> 00:08:25.470
So we can say,
hey, this person's

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:28.340
most qualified for the job
that you've just asked for.

00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:30.450
We're going to give that to you.

00:08:30.450 --> 00:08:32.010
So we can do that
very, very quickly.

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:34.929
So I guess stepping back a bit,
the traditional way of doing

00:08:34.929 --> 00:08:37.520
this with a crowdsourced
based system,

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:39.570
so something like
MTurk or CrowdFlower

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:42.460
would be you do the
call to Rainforest.

00:08:42.460 --> 00:08:44.510
Rainforest then does
a call to CrowdFlower,

00:08:44.510 --> 00:08:47.310
say, and says hey, list
a job on the surface.

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:49.107
And then we list a job there.

00:08:49.107 --> 00:08:51.440
Then the worker goes to the
page when they feel like it,

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:54.670
refreshes the page, sees
like a Craigslist style

00:08:54.670 --> 00:08:58.970
listing of things to do, clicks
on one, maybe decides to do it,

00:08:58.970 --> 00:09:00.290
and then does it.

00:09:00.290 --> 00:09:03.230
So that process
took, I don't know,

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:06.910
three to seven minutes to
do, maybe, traditionally,

00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:08.940
and then relied on workers
actually refreshing.

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:11.100
So we ended up building
notification services

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:12.170
on top of that.

00:09:12.170 --> 00:09:13.740
But then we realized
the easier way

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:16.114
to do it is just to make them
install a Chrome extension,

00:09:16.114 --> 00:09:18.310
or have them install
a Chrome extension.

00:09:18.310 --> 00:09:19.980
So this is a much
more fluid way.

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:22.680
So after pressing the
button or using the API

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:25.490
or doing it in a build,
it's generally a few seconds

00:09:25.490 --> 00:09:27.740
before we get the first
workers doing your work, which

00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:31.154
is pretty crazy when you
realize how many humans that is.

00:09:31.154 --> 00:09:33.070
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
that is pretty amazing.

00:09:33.070 --> 00:09:35.560
RUSSELL SMITH: We
accidentally DDoS-- not DDoS,

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:37.483
but we take people down.

00:09:37.483 --> 00:09:38.608
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh really?

00:09:38.608 --> 00:09:39.600
RUSSELL SMITH: Yes, so--

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:40.655
FRANCESC CAMPOY: You're
testing for functionality,

00:09:40.655 --> 00:09:42.937
but it's like oh yeah,
it doesn't scale.

00:09:42.937 --> 00:09:43.770
RUSSELL SMITH: Yeah.

00:09:43.770 --> 00:09:45.830
So I mean, that's one
of the things we've

00:09:45.830 --> 00:09:48.990
got to ship this quarter is
something to allow us to limit

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:52.440
the parallelism, because
I guess unlike, say,

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:54.030
if you are doing
testing traditionally

00:09:54.030 --> 00:10:00.502
with Selenium style stuff,
a lot of the crowd-- crowd?

00:10:00.502 --> 00:10:02.930
Cloud hosted providers
for that, maybe

00:10:02.930 --> 00:10:05.930
you'll get 64 parallel
things at once.

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:08.920
We can do a couple of
thousand things in parallel.

00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:12.920
So that tends to put a large
amount of load on your system,

00:10:12.920 --> 00:10:14.350
if you're not used to it.

00:10:14.350 --> 00:10:15.266
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:10:15.266 --> 00:10:17.270
So from an architectural
point of view,

00:10:17.270 --> 00:10:22.600
how does Rainforest QA
distribute all of these flows?

00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:24.710
Are these applications
that you're

00:10:24.710 --> 00:10:28.577
testing running on the customer
side and you manage the--

00:10:28.577 --> 00:10:29.410
RUSSELL SMITH: Yeah.

00:10:29.410 --> 00:10:32.200
Generally-- well in
fact 100% of cases,

00:10:32.200 --> 00:10:35.370
the application is running--
the customer runs it.

00:10:35.370 --> 00:10:37.490
And the main reason why is this.

00:10:37.490 --> 00:10:39.390
Well, you guys are developers.

00:10:39.390 --> 00:10:43.160
It's like so many different
ways of running something

00:10:43.160 --> 00:10:46.409
and so many different ways of
having dependencies and getting

00:10:46.409 --> 00:10:47.200
in the right state.

00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:49.887
It's just totally
non-trivial to do to right,

00:10:49.887 --> 00:10:51.300
to run somebody else's app.

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:54.240
So we let you run that.

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:58.210
And generally people deploy to
a staging or a QA environment

00:10:58.210 --> 00:11:00.629
and then point Rainforest
at that, works pretty well.

00:11:00.629 --> 00:11:02.170
If you're on Heroku,
you can actually

00:11:02.170 --> 00:11:06.430
use this feature code review
apps, which is pretty bad ass.

00:11:06.430 --> 00:11:08.647
If you do a pull request,
it will start an app

00:11:08.647 --> 00:11:10.980
with that code and then you
can point Rainforest at that

00:11:10.980 --> 00:11:12.160
as well.

00:11:12.160 --> 00:11:15.970
I know you were talking about
doing that for Google, right,

00:11:15.970 --> 00:11:17.054
for your App Engine stuff.

00:11:17.054 --> 00:11:19.678
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, trying to
implement something like that.

00:11:19.678 --> 00:11:20.500
And it is fun.

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:21.930
And I don't think
it is that hard.

00:11:21.930 --> 00:11:24.140
So maybe we will be
able to share some news.

00:11:24.140 --> 00:11:24.950
RUSSELL SMITH: What suspense.

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:26.760
MARK MANDEL: Well you could use
an App Engine version manually.

00:11:26.760 --> 00:11:27.676
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:11:27.676 --> 00:11:30.550
So what I'm trying to do
is do it automatically.

00:11:30.550 --> 00:11:31.300
It could be fun.

00:11:31.300 --> 00:11:34.066
RUSSELL SMITH: Yeah,
you could automate that.

00:11:34.066 --> 00:11:36.440
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So that is
about all the web integration

00:11:36.440 --> 00:11:36.940
test.

00:11:36.940 --> 00:11:38.610
Like all of that
is about the web.

00:11:38.610 --> 00:11:40.105
What about mobile, mobile first?

00:11:40.105 --> 00:11:42.230
RUSSELL SMITH: Yeah, actually
there's a good point.

00:11:42.230 --> 00:11:47.340
We launched-- I guess we've
been running mobile for,

00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:49.230
I don't know, maybe
five or six months now

00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:52.950
for a few customers,
maybe 10 customers.

00:11:52.950 --> 00:11:55.940
And then last week we launched
a self-serve version of this

00:11:55.940 --> 00:11:58.520
that anyone can sign
up to today, where

00:11:58.520 --> 00:11:59.980
you prepay for
credits rather than

00:11:59.980 --> 00:12:02.380
enter into a contract with us.

00:12:02.380 --> 00:12:05.550
So yeah, we're powered
by Amazon's Device Farm.

00:12:05.550 --> 00:12:07.130
I see you guys cringing.

00:12:07.130 --> 00:12:10.640
You guys didn't do iOS fast
enough on your Firebase stuff,

00:12:10.640 --> 00:12:12.690
so we had to go with them.

00:12:12.690 --> 00:12:14.190
But they're awesome.

00:12:14.190 --> 00:12:18.450
So they give us real
devices, like direct-- well,

00:12:18.450 --> 00:12:20.580
I say direct, so they
give us real devices.

00:12:20.580 --> 00:12:25.890
But the way they're designed
was for running automated tests.

00:12:25.890 --> 00:12:27.600
So because we're
powered by humans,

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:30.390
we actually need to
give the device to them,

00:12:30.390 --> 00:12:32.196
as in present it to the tester.

00:12:32.196 --> 00:12:34.094
And it wasn't really
set up for that.

00:12:34.094 --> 00:12:35.760
So we had to build
some pretty cool tech

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:40.540
to allow us to basically
transport the device

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:41.885
into the person's browser.

00:12:41.885 --> 00:12:46.330
So the short version is we wrote
a VNC server and an SSH server

00:12:46.330 --> 00:12:49.990
and some robust tunneling
magic that goes into the app

00:12:49.990 --> 00:12:51.700
so we can inject into you app.

00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:54.010
And then when your
app starts from there,

00:12:54.010 --> 00:12:55.570
we can reverse
tunnel back into it

00:12:55.570 --> 00:12:57.370
and take control
of the phone, which

00:12:57.370 --> 00:13:00.090
is kind of awesome because it
turns out actually it works,

00:13:00.090 --> 00:13:03.040
even though it
sounds totally crazy.

00:13:03.040 --> 00:13:06.900
But it just means we can run
like 100, 200 devices at once

00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:08.525
and give them out to
testers instantly.

00:13:08.525 --> 00:13:13.750
So super low friction for you
as an end user, as a customer.

00:13:13.750 --> 00:13:16.640
Also you, again, writing
tests in plain English,

00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:19.820
so like low brittleness
of your test,

00:13:19.820 --> 00:13:21.880
and also super easy
to get started.

00:13:21.880 --> 00:13:25.124
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So with
all of these testers and all

00:13:25.124 --> 00:13:27.665
the testing devices that you
can be running at the same time,

00:13:27.665 --> 00:13:29.910
I'm imagining that
you are generating

00:13:29.910 --> 00:13:32.820
a pretty decent amount of data.

00:13:32.820 --> 00:13:34.355
How do you how do
you handle that?

00:13:34.355 --> 00:13:39.870
Do you create a-- do I get
an email saying passed?

00:13:39.870 --> 00:13:42.060
RUSSELL SMITH: Yeah--
well actually kind of.

00:13:42.060 --> 00:13:43.750
So at a high level,
yeah, you'll get

00:13:43.750 --> 00:13:46.660
a-- there's a bunch of
ways of notifying you.

00:13:46.660 --> 00:13:48.460
But one of the
classic ones is email.

00:13:48.460 --> 00:13:51.370
So you will get an e-mail
that just says passed.

00:13:51.370 --> 00:13:54.305
But then it lists all
the tests that passed.

00:13:54.305 --> 00:13:56.180
The interesting one is
when you get failures.

00:13:56.180 --> 00:13:59.270
Like we'll send you the failure
and what browser or device

00:13:59.270 --> 00:14:00.600
it failed on.

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:02.840
And then if you
click on the link,

00:14:02.840 --> 00:14:06.570
you can go down into
seeing what the tester did,

00:14:06.570 --> 00:14:09.900
whether that's through
screenshots or device

00:14:09.900 --> 00:14:12.950
logs for iOS stuff.

00:14:12.950 --> 00:14:16.050
Or for web stuff, we'll
give you half format,

00:14:16.050 --> 00:14:18.560
which is-- I guess you
guys at Google people,

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:21.120
so it's a format I
think invented here

00:14:21.120 --> 00:14:27.389
that Chrome exports from
the Network Debug panel.

00:14:27.389 --> 00:14:29.180
So anyway, we'll give
you that same format,

00:14:29.180 --> 00:14:31.096
which means you can use
some of the same tools

00:14:31.096 --> 00:14:33.770
to introspect it and
figure out what's going on.

00:14:33.770 --> 00:14:35.640
So you can get the
actual logs, because we

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:37.570
man in the middle
our own machines

00:14:37.570 --> 00:14:40.710
to be able to track
all the requests that

00:14:40.710 --> 00:14:44.415
go through-- all the
requests that the testers do.

00:14:44.415 --> 00:14:45.290
Does that make sense?

00:14:45.290 --> 00:14:45.680
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:14:45.680 --> 00:14:46.596
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:14:46.596 --> 00:14:49.440
RUSSELL SMITH: As for where we
store that data, a bunch of it

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:51.120
is in Postgres.

00:14:51.120 --> 00:14:56.090
Some of it now is going
into Google BigQuery.

00:14:56.090 --> 00:14:57.950
And then a bunch of
it is stored on S3.

00:14:57.950 --> 00:15:00.642
So the staticy stuff that
doesn't change is stored on S3.

00:15:00.642 --> 00:15:02.850
MARK MANDEL: So you said
some of it goes to BigQuery.

00:15:02.850 --> 00:15:06.060
Now you were sharing a very
large number with us earlier.

00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:08.080
Is that really just
some of the data?

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:10.163
RUSSELL SMITH: Yeah, that's
just some of the data.

00:15:10.163 --> 00:15:13.250
So short version,
we are having--

00:15:13.250 --> 00:15:15.740
we use Heroku like a lot.

00:15:15.740 --> 00:15:17.610
Almost all of our
infrastructure's

00:15:17.610 --> 00:15:20.430
on Heroku, apart from
the machines that provide

00:15:20.430 --> 00:15:22.160
virtual machines to testers.

00:15:22.160 --> 00:15:24.110
They're in a different place.

00:15:24.110 --> 00:15:28.430
But Heroku has a super
little known feature

00:15:28.430 --> 00:15:30.110
called log drains.

00:15:30.110 --> 00:15:32.920
And log drains let
you pipe log output

00:15:32.920 --> 00:15:36.190
from the app and all
the processes of the app

00:15:36.190 --> 00:15:38.300
to anywhere you want.

00:15:38.300 --> 00:15:40.920
So traditionally that
only support Syslog,

00:15:40.920 --> 00:15:43.710
but they added an
undocumented, or I

00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:45.340
think at the time
undocumented feature

00:15:45.340 --> 00:15:48.250
where you could do
HTTP based transport.

00:15:48.250 --> 00:15:54.610
So I spoke with you guys maybe
a year or two ago, a year ago

00:15:54.610 --> 00:15:57.820
maybe, and figured
out, hey, I'm just

00:15:57.820 --> 00:16:00.650
going to write a Go program
that uses the streaming

00:16:00.650 --> 00:16:04.070
API for BigQuery and
just takes in all

00:16:04.070 --> 00:16:06.020
the logs from our
entire infrastructure

00:16:06.020 --> 00:16:08.320
and puts them into BigQuery.

00:16:08.320 --> 00:16:10.910
So now the cool thing is we're
having a bunch of problems

00:16:10.910 --> 00:16:13.770
with our logging provider, who
I'm guessing I should probably

00:16:13.770 --> 00:16:17.190
not name because I don't
want any politics here.

00:16:17.190 --> 00:16:19.390
But we are looking
for a good provider

00:16:19.390 --> 00:16:20.980
one, that was fast
and searchable

00:16:20.980 --> 00:16:24.070
and didn't have bad
retention and didn't cost us

00:16:24.070 --> 00:16:28.610
an absolute fortune a year,
and then realized actually,

00:16:28.610 --> 00:16:29.870
there aren't any.

00:16:29.870 --> 00:16:33.110
By the way, we've since found
logdna.com, which I highly

00:16:33.110 --> 00:16:34.070
recommend those guys.

00:16:34.070 --> 00:16:35.410
They're cheap.

00:16:35.410 --> 00:16:37.730
They do a good job.

00:16:37.730 --> 00:16:39.830
They're not super lots
of bells and whistles,

00:16:39.830 --> 00:16:42.880
but that's the
tradeoff with them.

00:16:42.880 --> 00:16:45.820
They just do what you
need, which is search.

00:16:45.820 --> 00:16:49.030
But anyway, we still
use BigQuery for this.

00:16:49.030 --> 00:16:51.725
So Go program,
streaming API, takes

00:16:51.725 --> 00:16:53.600
in all the logs from
our whole infrastructure

00:16:53.600 --> 00:16:55.470
and just puts it
straight into a table.

00:16:55.470 --> 00:16:58.639
It also doesn't extra
nice stuff for parsing out

00:16:58.639 --> 00:17:01.180
the different fields and putting
them into the right columns.

00:17:01.180 --> 00:17:07.000
But yeah, we've got about 25
billion log lines in BigQuery

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:07.680
now.

00:17:07.680 --> 00:17:08.607
So it's kind of cool.

00:17:08.607 --> 00:17:09.940
MARK MANDEL: That's really cool.

00:17:09.940 --> 00:17:12.510
Do you know how much
do you tend to ingest?

00:17:12.510 --> 00:17:16.180
Can you share that with us,
like over a set time period?

00:17:16.180 --> 00:17:19.650
RUSSELL SMITH: It's normally
a floor of like a 1,000

00:17:19.650 --> 00:17:21.230
rows a seconds kind of thing.

00:17:21.230 --> 00:17:23.319
And then it goes up
depending on traffic.

00:17:23.319 --> 00:17:25.724
So that's normally our
baseline for Rainforest.

00:17:25.724 --> 00:17:28.140
Maybe it's a bit lower at the
weekend, but during the week

00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:29.960
it's about that.

00:17:29.960 --> 00:17:33.990
So it's not super high
log wise, I guess.

00:17:33.990 --> 00:17:34.879
It spikes though.

00:17:34.879 --> 00:17:37.170
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, I
mean, 1000 requests per second

00:17:37.170 --> 00:17:40.260
is pretty decent, to be honest.

00:17:40.260 --> 00:17:42.457
RUSSELL SMITH: So
triply, the program,

00:17:42.457 --> 00:17:44.290
A, I've been meaning
to open source forever,

00:17:44.290 --> 00:17:46.670
so I should probably
actually do.

00:17:46.670 --> 00:17:51.510
But B, it runs on Heroku,
so it's like self.

00:17:51.510 --> 00:17:54.640
It's kind of weird, Heroku
logs going to Heroku,

00:17:54.640 --> 00:17:55.950
then going to you guys.

00:17:55.950 --> 00:17:58.262
So yeah, super cool.

00:17:58.262 --> 00:17:59.470
MARK MANDEL: And that's cool.

00:17:59.470 --> 00:18:01.169
So once the data's
in BigQuery, what

00:18:01.169 --> 00:18:02.710
are you actually
doing with it there?

00:18:02.710 --> 00:18:04.340
RUSSELL SMITH:
Yeah, good question.

00:18:04.340 --> 00:18:06.220
So we have a bunch
of standard queries

00:18:06.220 --> 00:18:08.080
that we run to find some issues.

00:18:08.080 --> 00:18:10.820
So we either run them
directly in your dashboard

00:18:10.820 --> 00:18:12.380
or we use something
called Redash,

00:18:12.380 --> 00:18:15.060
which I think you
actually recommended to me

00:18:15.060 --> 00:18:15.870
like a while ago.

00:18:15.870 --> 00:18:17.850
But Redash, for those
of you that don't know,

00:18:17.850 --> 00:18:21.370
is kind of like open
source dashboarding system.

00:18:21.370 --> 00:18:23.410
But it lets you run
queries on schedules

00:18:23.410 --> 00:18:26.420
and it connects to
Postgres, Mongo, MySQL,

00:18:26.420 --> 00:18:30.450
but also BigQuery and
Redshift, et cetera, et cetera,

00:18:30.450 --> 00:18:31.110
etc. cetera.

00:18:31.110 --> 00:18:32.640
So it's super cool for that.

00:18:32.640 --> 00:18:33.320
So we use that.

00:18:33.320 --> 00:18:35.450
So we have a bunch of
queries that run regularly

00:18:35.450 --> 00:18:37.600
to find certain things
that we're looking forward,

00:18:37.600 --> 00:18:39.160
do stats.

00:18:39.160 --> 00:18:42.930
And it's super awesome because
it's cheap to run on you guys,

00:18:42.930 --> 00:18:45.370
and it's also fast.

00:18:45.370 --> 00:18:49.582
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So having
that amount of rows on BigQuery

00:18:49.582 --> 00:18:52.675
I'm wondering,
how do you manage?

00:18:52.675 --> 00:18:55.790
Do you actually have a
bunch of different tables?

00:18:55.790 --> 00:18:59.179
It's just one humongous
table with billions of rows?

00:18:59.179 --> 00:18:59.970
RUSSELL SMITH: LOL.

00:18:59.970 --> 00:19:03.790
This is the noob mistake,
which I need to fix,

00:19:03.790 --> 00:19:07.330
which I was reading the
docs about recently.

00:19:07.330 --> 00:19:09.570
When I first started,
I didn't realize

00:19:09.570 --> 00:19:13.960
that we should do named
date version tables.

00:19:13.960 --> 00:19:16.040
Turns out at least
in the past that

00:19:16.040 --> 00:19:17.900
was impossible to
fix without just

00:19:17.900 --> 00:19:20.170
making a new set of tables,
which I didn't really

00:19:20.170 --> 00:19:21.800
want to do.

00:19:21.800 --> 00:19:23.650
So I think I read
the docs correctly

00:19:23.650 --> 00:19:26.270
saying that I can
actually do that now

00:19:26.270 --> 00:19:29.559
without taking it offline,
which looks kind of cool.

00:19:29.559 --> 00:19:31.100
So anyway, I haven't
got around to it

00:19:31.100 --> 00:19:35.260
because the cost is actually low
enough that I don't care, yet.

00:19:35.260 --> 00:19:36.510
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That is nice.

00:19:36.510 --> 00:19:38.509
RUSSELL SMITH: I'm kind
of attracted by the fact

00:19:38.509 --> 00:19:40.800
that if the data is still
for a month or something,

00:19:40.800 --> 00:19:41.980
it's like half the cost.

00:19:41.980 --> 00:19:43.280
Is that correct?

00:19:43.280 --> 00:19:46.940
There's some discount if
the data doesn't get added

00:19:46.940 --> 00:19:48.477
to after a period of time.

00:19:48.477 --> 00:19:49.810
Anyway, most of our [INAUDIBLE].

00:19:49.810 --> 00:19:50.670
FRANCESC CAMPOY: We
should check that out.

00:19:50.670 --> 00:19:51.530
I'm not sure.

00:19:51.530 --> 00:19:54.610
RUSSELL SMITH: No, I was digging
around the BigQuery docs,

00:19:54.610 --> 00:19:56.820
so I'm pretty sure
that's correct.

00:19:56.820 --> 00:19:58.847
But Google it yourself.

00:19:58.847 --> 00:20:00.430
MARK MANDEL: Yes,
so if a table is not

00:20:00.430 --> 00:20:03.330
edited for 90 consecutive
days, the price of storage

00:20:03.330 --> 00:20:08.096
that table automatically drops
by 50% to $0.01 per gigabyte--

00:20:08.096 --> 00:20:09.720
RUSSELL SMITH: Which
is totally insane.

00:20:09.720 --> 00:20:11.130
MARK MANDEL: $0.01 per
gigabyte per month.

00:20:11.130 --> 00:20:11.410
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh wow.

00:20:11.410 --> 00:20:12.535
RUSSELL SMITH: It's insane.

00:20:12.535 --> 00:20:13.544
And it's also awesome.

00:20:13.544 --> 00:20:15.210
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That
is pretty awesome.

00:20:15.210 --> 00:20:17.099
We learned BigQuery from him.

00:20:17.099 --> 00:20:18.890
MARK MANDEL: That's
why we do this podcast.

00:20:18.890 --> 00:20:20.550
FRANCESC CAMPOY: We're
not very good at this.

00:20:20.550 --> 00:20:22.310
RUSSELL SMITH: So I need to
switch to partition tables

00:20:22.310 --> 00:20:23.840
so that we can take
advantage of [INAUDIBLE].

00:20:23.840 --> 00:20:24.730
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, that way--

00:20:24.730 --> 00:20:26.230
RUSSELL SMITH: No,
we have one table

00:20:26.230 --> 00:20:29.190
with nearly 25 billion rows in.

00:20:29.190 --> 00:20:32.450
And it's working,
which is kind of cool.

00:20:32.450 --> 00:20:34.670
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Out of curiosity,

00:20:34.670 --> 00:20:38.830
how long does it take to execute
the most complex query you

00:20:38.830 --> 00:20:40.420
have?

00:20:40.420 --> 00:20:43.610
RUSSELL SMITH: So
normally we don't really

00:20:43.610 --> 00:20:45.040
run queries over
the full data set

00:20:45.040 --> 00:20:46.700
unless there's some
security thing that we're

00:20:46.700 --> 00:20:46.790
trying to investigate.

00:20:46.790 --> 00:20:48.750
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That
is probably a good idea.

00:20:48.750 --> 00:20:51.200
RUSSELL SMITH: And there's
this awesome thing in BigQuery

00:20:51.200 --> 00:20:56.520
where you can scope not
by data in the table,

00:20:56.520 --> 00:20:58.810
but by when that
data was inserted.

00:20:58.810 --> 00:21:00.780
So almost all our
queries-- I can't

00:21:00.780 --> 00:21:01.990
remember what it's called.

00:21:01.990 --> 00:21:04.150
Anyway, almost all our
queries are done by that,

00:21:04.150 --> 00:21:06.690
so depending on
what you're doing,

00:21:06.690 --> 00:21:08.580
they're normally
either a day or weeks

00:21:08.580 --> 00:21:11.500
worth of stuff, which I think
is actually the max for that.

00:21:11.500 --> 00:21:14.230
But only on rare
circumstances do

00:21:14.230 --> 00:21:16.982
we query all of the data ever.

00:21:16.982 --> 00:21:19.440
So it's normally if we have
something that somebody's like,

00:21:19.440 --> 00:21:21.574
oh, this thing happened
two months ago.

00:21:21.574 --> 00:21:22.240
Can you help us?

00:21:22.240 --> 00:21:24.590
I'm like, yeah, maybe we can.

00:21:24.590 --> 00:21:27.590
Turns out-- so yeah,
we don't normally

00:21:27.590 --> 00:21:28.710
query the whole data set.

00:21:28.710 --> 00:21:31.390
So things are pretty quick,
like normally sub a minute.

00:21:31.390 --> 00:21:33.400
I mean, we queried
earlier to find out

00:21:33.400 --> 00:21:35.310
how many rows were
in the table today,

00:21:35.310 --> 00:21:37.226
and that was the longest
where I've ever seen,

00:21:37.226 --> 00:21:39.580
which was just over a minute.

00:21:39.580 --> 00:21:42.640
And that's because it counted
every single thing ever,

00:21:42.640 --> 00:21:46.750
which is kind of still
really cool, 68 seconds.

00:21:46.750 --> 00:21:48.470
FRANCESC CAMPOY: 68
seconds to count--

00:21:48.470 --> 00:21:49.380
RUSSELL SMITH: 25 billion rows.

00:21:49.380 --> 00:21:51.380
FRANCESC CAMPOY: 25 billion
rows, that is not bad.

00:21:51.380 --> 00:21:52.255
RUSSELL SMITH: But I
can live with that.

00:21:52.255 --> 00:21:54.190
MARK MANDEL: So you're talking
about your visualizations

00:21:54.190 --> 00:21:56.920
and you're saying you were
using it to find certain issues

00:21:56.920 --> 00:21:58.000
and things.

00:21:58.000 --> 00:21:59.610
Can you talk a little bit
more, like what sort of issues

00:21:59.610 --> 00:22:01.290
that you're looking for or
how you're using that in--

00:22:01.290 --> 00:22:02.430
RUSSELL SMITH: In
the visualizations?

00:22:02.430 --> 00:22:03.320
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:22:03.320 --> 00:22:05.653
RUSSELL SMITH: So I don't
know, we're looking at trends.

00:22:05.653 --> 00:22:09.782
So we're also using
another awesome product

00:22:09.782 --> 00:22:11.240
to do this, which
seems to actually

00:22:11.240 --> 00:22:13.530
be the better way of doing it.

00:22:13.530 --> 00:22:19.220
Short version, we're looking at
trends in memory usage or load.

00:22:19.220 --> 00:22:21.530
Can we spot any trends
that are outliers

00:22:21.530 --> 00:22:23.250
and then dig into
why this happened?

00:22:23.250 --> 00:22:25.690
So we're actually
using a service

00:22:25.690 --> 00:22:31.090
called Honeycomb.io, which
also, I think, is in beta,

00:22:31.090 --> 00:22:34.350
but can super
recommend those guys.

00:22:34.350 --> 00:22:36.680
Short version,
we're trying to find

00:22:36.680 --> 00:22:40.470
like, hey, this bunch
of requests was slow.

00:22:40.470 --> 00:22:42.090
Why was that?

00:22:42.090 --> 00:22:44.880
And then for instance, we were
investigating the other day

00:22:44.880 --> 00:22:48.020
and found one of
our diners, which

00:22:48.020 --> 00:22:51.950
I guess Roku parlance for like
a machine running a process,

00:22:51.950 --> 00:22:56.840
was using way more swap
than all the other machines

00:22:56.840 --> 00:22:58.440
for no apparent reason.

00:22:58.440 --> 00:23:02.040
So digging into that,
even finding that

00:23:02.040 --> 00:23:05.866
was only possible using
BigQuery and Honeycomb.

00:23:05.866 --> 00:23:07.240
MARK MANDEL: So
most of your data

00:23:07.240 --> 00:23:11.429
really is about machine
analytics and stuff like that.

00:23:11.429 --> 00:23:12.970
RUSSELL SMITH: Yeah,
we haven't moved

00:23:12.970 --> 00:23:16.220
to-- there's another thing
that we're working on,

00:23:16.220 --> 00:23:19.180
which is to move
some of our data

00:23:19.180 --> 00:23:21.370
or a lot of our most
important tables

00:23:21.370 --> 00:23:24.730
from Postgres into BigQuery.

00:23:24.730 --> 00:23:29.280
And the main reason we want to
do that is for doing analytics,

00:23:29.280 --> 00:23:33.800
so finding out-- turns
out we have too much data

00:23:33.800 --> 00:23:38.180
to be able to query the entire
thing at a reasonable speed

00:23:38.180 --> 00:23:39.560
on Postgres.

00:23:39.560 --> 00:23:41.430
So we've got, I
don't know, coming up

00:23:41.430 --> 00:23:44.760
to 300 or 400 gigs of data,
which actually doesn't seem

00:23:44.760 --> 00:23:48.000
like that much, but
turns out is enough it's

00:23:48.000 --> 00:23:51.180
expensive to run at
a fast speed when

00:23:51.180 --> 00:23:54.270
we're doing stupid queries.

00:23:54.270 --> 00:23:56.430
So anyway, short
version, we're working

00:23:56.430 --> 00:24:00.700
on a way of exporting that data
direct into BigQuery, which

00:24:00.700 --> 00:24:05.180
will mean that we can do the
more insane queries faster,

00:24:05.180 --> 00:24:06.880
which is going to be cool.

00:24:06.880 --> 00:24:08.830
Maybe we'll open
source that as well.

00:24:08.830 --> 00:24:10.367
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, you should.

00:24:10.367 --> 00:24:12.700
RUSSELL SMITH: Should open
source the first one as well.

00:24:12.700 --> 00:24:15.240
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, and
write a blog post you said.

00:24:15.240 --> 00:24:17.230
RUSSELL SMITH: Good point.

00:24:17.230 --> 00:24:19.180
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So now that you've

00:24:19.180 --> 00:24:23.490
been using BigQuery for a
while, and as you were saying,

00:24:23.490 --> 00:24:25.730
you had some noob mistakes,
like having just one

00:24:25.730 --> 00:24:27.830
huge table for everything.

00:24:27.830 --> 00:24:30.660
Do you have any other
piece of advice for people

00:24:30.660 --> 00:24:32.750
that are starting to migrate?

00:24:32.750 --> 00:24:35.440
They have like a huge
database relational,

00:24:35.440 --> 00:24:37.928
and they're like oh,
should I move to BigQuery?

00:24:37.928 --> 00:24:38.761
RUSSELL SMITH: Sure.

00:24:41.420 --> 00:24:44.090
Basically the biggest
limitation when

00:24:44.090 --> 00:24:48.290
I started doing it was-- well,
I guess I did read the docs,

00:24:48.290 --> 00:24:50.600
but I just didn't bother
doing a bunch of stuff

00:24:50.600 --> 00:24:52.220
because I wanted to get started.

00:24:52.220 --> 00:24:54.190
But the biggest
thing I would say now

00:24:54.190 --> 00:24:56.080
is make sure you read
the docs properly

00:24:56.080 --> 00:24:58.620
and probably go with the
standard C core stuff, which

00:24:58.620 --> 00:25:00.510
seems to be the new version.

00:25:00.510 --> 00:25:02.760
I'm still on the older version.

00:25:02.760 --> 00:25:04.940
The standard SQL
seems super awesome

00:25:04.940 --> 00:25:08.030
because it removes one of the
major limitations of BigQuery,

00:25:08.030 --> 00:25:11.600
which is updating
stuff, which now you

00:25:11.600 --> 00:25:14.420
can treat it almost
like a proper-- eh,

00:25:14.420 --> 00:25:15.682
almost like a proper database.

00:25:15.682 --> 00:25:17.390
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
that is actually--

00:25:17.390 --> 00:25:19.780
RUSSELL SMITH: I don't mean
that meanly by the way.

00:25:19.780 --> 00:25:21.870
It's not like just
append only now.

00:25:21.870 --> 00:25:23.810
You can actually update things.

00:25:23.810 --> 00:25:25.685
FRANCESC CAMPOY: For
quite a long time, yeah,

00:25:25.685 --> 00:25:27.799
BigQuery was only append only.

00:25:27.799 --> 00:25:30.340
I mean, totally makes sense if
you're talking about something

00:25:30.340 --> 00:25:31.150
to analyze logs.

00:25:31.150 --> 00:25:32.730
RUSSELL SMITH: It totally
makes sense [INAUDIBLE]--

00:25:32.730 --> 00:25:33.230
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Modifying logs--

00:25:33.230 --> 00:25:34.605
RUSSELL SMITH:
--use case for us,

00:25:34.605 --> 00:25:36.840
but horrible use case for
the analytics stuff for us

00:25:36.840 --> 00:25:39.227
because we need to
drop it every time we

00:25:39.227 --> 00:25:40.810
want to update it
again, which I guess

00:25:40.810 --> 00:25:43.940
is also possible, just-- yeah.

00:25:43.940 --> 00:25:46.365
I would say definitely
look at the streaming API.

00:25:46.365 --> 00:25:48.130
It's pretty awesome.

00:25:48.130 --> 00:25:50.800
It also means
stuff's really fast.

00:25:50.800 --> 00:25:52.450
There's a really good
Go library for it

00:25:52.450 --> 00:25:55.050
that I found which I will
get put in the podcast notes.

00:25:55.050 --> 00:25:57.300
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, we'll
put it on the show notes.

00:25:57.300 --> 00:25:58.133
RUSSELL SMITH: Yeah.

00:25:58.133 --> 00:26:01.390
And then I guess the
only other thing-- what

00:26:01.390 --> 00:26:02.750
do you want to do to wrap up?

00:26:02.750 --> 00:26:05.810
The only other thing I was
going say was I'm in reInvent.

00:26:05.810 --> 00:26:08.140
I'm speaking at
reInvent about MTurk.

00:26:08.140 --> 00:26:10.710
So if anyone wants to
come say hi, do that.

00:26:10.710 --> 00:26:13.290
I'll also be at the
CTO summit in November.

00:26:13.290 --> 00:26:15.584
So if anyone wants to
say hi there, do it.

00:26:15.584 --> 00:26:16.500
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:26:16.500 --> 00:26:19.690
Yeah, go find him and
ask him about BigQuery.

00:26:19.690 --> 00:26:22.854
RUSSELL SMITH: Yeah, or Turk
or any crowdsourcing stuff.

00:26:22.854 --> 00:26:23.770
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:26:23.770 --> 00:26:25.950
Well thank you so much
for taking the time

00:26:25.950 --> 00:26:28.200
to talk with us.

00:26:28.200 --> 00:26:30.740
That's a very
interesting interview.

00:26:30.740 --> 00:26:34.730
I will have a bunch of links
to all the different projects

00:26:34.730 --> 00:26:35.960
that you have mentioned.

00:26:35.960 --> 00:26:38.210
And hopefully we'll also
have links to the open source

00:26:38.210 --> 00:26:41.094
versions of your [INAUDIBLE].

00:26:41.094 --> 00:26:42.510
RUSSELL SMITH: I
just hired a VPN,

00:26:42.510 --> 00:26:45.230
so I should actually have some
time to do this kind of stuff

00:26:45.230 --> 00:26:45.400
now.

00:26:45.400 --> 00:26:45.690
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Awesome.

00:26:45.690 --> 00:26:46.400
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:26:46.400 --> 00:26:47.270
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Well thank you.

00:26:47.270 --> 00:26:47.680
RUSSELL SMITH: Cool.

00:26:47.680 --> 00:26:48.247
Thanks guys.

00:26:48.247 --> 00:26:49.080
MARK MANDEL: Thanks.

00:26:49.080 --> 00:26:51.880
It was a pleasure and a joy to
have Russell Smith on with us

00:26:51.880 --> 00:26:52.510
today.

00:26:52.510 --> 00:26:54.630
I definitely appreciate
hearing about people

00:26:54.630 --> 00:26:57.180
building interesting things,
especially I think CrossCloud.

00:26:57.180 --> 00:26:58.555
I think that's
really interesting

00:26:58.555 --> 00:27:01.269
to see how people are
doing that sort of stuff.

00:27:01.269 --> 00:27:02.060
It was really good.

00:27:02.060 --> 00:27:03.820
And the amount of data he's
processing, it's decent.

00:27:03.820 --> 00:27:04.770
It's a decent amount.

00:27:04.770 --> 00:27:06.150
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It is.

00:27:06.150 --> 00:27:09.386
When he was walking away,
we had a quick chat.

00:27:09.386 --> 00:27:11.010
And one of the things
that I was saying

00:27:11.010 --> 00:27:14.640
was like, oh, so you
have 25 billion rows.

00:27:14.640 --> 00:27:16.130
Are they very short rows?

00:27:16.130 --> 00:27:20.410
He's like, oh no, they have
like tens or more fields.

00:27:20.410 --> 00:27:21.980
I was like, oh wow, OK.

00:27:21.980 --> 00:27:23.899
And some of the fields
have JSON inside.

00:27:23.899 --> 00:27:25.440
So it was like decent
amount of data.

00:27:25.440 --> 00:27:26.450
So I was like wow.

00:27:26.450 --> 00:27:27.900
Yeah, very cool.

00:27:27.900 --> 00:27:31.490
So yeah, looking forward
to all the open sourcing

00:27:31.490 --> 00:27:32.270
of those tools.

00:27:32.270 --> 00:27:33.730
I'm very excited
about that, especially

00:27:33.730 --> 00:27:34.710
because they're written in Go.

00:27:34.710 --> 00:27:35.917
MARK MANDEL: Yes, very good.

00:27:35.917 --> 00:27:37.000
It'll make you very happy.

00:27:37.000 --> 00:27:37.666
All right, cool.

00:27:37.666 --> 00:27:40.060
So let's get into our question--
or apparently questions

00:27:40.060 --> 00:27:41.289
of the week.

00:27:41.289 --> 00:27:42.830
Yeah, you raised
the first one, which

00:27:42.830 --> 00:27:46.030
I think is an interesting
one and got me very excited,

00:27:46.030 --> 00:27:48.495
which is how to react to
email from Google Cloud.

00:27:48.495 --> 00:27:52.650
So say I, I don't know--
this is always a fun one.

00:27:52.650 --> 00:27:54.810
I'm going to get sent an
email by some third party,

00:27:54.810 --> 00:27:56.669
[INAUDIBLE] for
example, and I want

00:27:56.669 --> 00:27:58.710
to be able to automatically
do something about it

00:27:58.710 --> 00:28:00.150
once I receive it.

00:28:00.150 --> 00:28:02.060
How can I find out
when that email

00:28:02.060 --> 00:28:04.885
comes to me so I can pass it
data and do stuff like that?

00:28:04.885 --> 00:28:06.510
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
this actually comes

00:28:06.510 --> 00:28:09.910
from a customer that
asked me this question.

00:28:09.910 --> 00:28:11.340
And it was pretty
fun because they

00:28:11.340 --> 00:28:15.260
were like, they need to
do something very complex

00:28:15.260 --> 00:28:17.830
on Compute Engine.

00:28:17.830 --> 00:28:21.180
And that very complex job needs
to be done every single time

00:28:21.180 --> 00:28:23.480
that someone could
update a database that

00:28:23.480 --> 00:28:27.520
was publicly available,
built by NASA actually.

00:28:27.520 --> 00:28:30.110
And the impressive
thing is that the way

00:28:30.110 --> 00:28:32.770
they're going to notify
people about there's

00:28:32.770 --> 00:28:35.452
new data in the database
is by sending emails.

00:28:35.452 --> 00:28:36.410
MARK MANDEL: Of course.

00:28:36.410 --> 00:28:37.980
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So yeah.

00:28:37.980 --> 00:28:40.790
So you had someone that was
receiving that e-mail that

00:28:40.790 --> 00:28:44.254
then could go and run a script
that could start everything.

00:28:44.254 --> 00:28:46.420
And I was like, I'm pretty
sure there's a better way

00:28:46.420 --> 00:28:47.820
to do this.

00:28:47.820 --> 00:28:49.900
And there's many
ways of doing it.

00:28:49.900 --> 00:28:53.320
One of them could be run
your own web server--

00:28:53.320 --> 00:28:54.669
run your own mail server.

00:28:54.669 --> 00:28:55.960
MARK MANDEL: Let's not do that.

00:28:55.960 --> 00:28:57.420
That doesn't sound like fun.

00:28:57.420 --> 00:28:59.700
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
that is definitely not fun.

00:28:59.700 --> 00:29:03.430
Then another thing you
could do is basically

00:29:03.430 --> 00:29:07.530
use IMAP to connect from your
script that runs regularly,

00:29:07.530 --> 00:29:10.314
like for instance using
Cron, and every hour

00:29:10.314 --> 00:29:11.480
checks if there's new email.

00:29:11.480 --> 00:29:13.271
If there's an email,
it starts doing stuff.

00:29:13.271 --> 00:29:15.620
But then the problem is
how often do you check?

00:29:15.620 --> 00:29:16.540
MARK MANDEL: You're
basically polling.

00:29:16.540 --> 00:29:18.790
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, so
you're polling all the time.

00:29:18.790 --> 00:29:20.090
So it's not perfect.

00:29:20.090 --> 00:29:23.550
And there's actually a
very, very easy solution,

00:29:23.550 --> 00:29:26.450
which is just using App Engine.

00:29:26.450 --> 00:29:28.710
MARK MANDEL: So App
Engine can receive email?

00:29:28.710 --> 00:29:29.626
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:29:29.626 --> 00:29:31.190
So you actually
get the same way--

00:29:31.190 --> 00:29:35.330
you get a URL to host
your web server, which

00:29:35.330 --> 00:29:38.080
is like yourappid.appspot.com.

00:29:38.080 --> 00:29:40.510
You also get an email,
which is whatever you want,

00:29:40.510 --> 00:29:41.718
at yourappid.appspotmail.com.

00:29:44.130 --> 00:29:47.510
So if you send an email to
that address, what will happen

00:29:47.510 --> 00:29:55.150
is that the email will be
sent as a POST HTTP request

00:29:55.150 --> 00:29:56.870
to your application.

00:29:56.870 --> 00:29:58.704
So now if your instance
is not running,

00:29:58.704 --> 00:30:01.120
like your application is not
running because you don't how

00:30:01.120 --> 00:30:03.910
any traffic-- which is probably
normal if you're only receiving

00:30:03.910 --> 00:30:06.150
mail once a day--
what will happen

00:30:06.150 --> 00:30:07.982
is that when you
receive that request,

00:30:07.982 --> 00:30:10.910
your new instance will start,
and you will treat that,

00:30:10.910 --> 00:30:12.330
and then you do
whatever you want.

00:30:12.330 --> 00:30:13.788
And one of the
things that you can,

00:30:13.788 --> 00:30:16.120
as we said at the
beginning of the podcast,

00:30:16.120 --> 00:30:19.620
was that you can start a
new Compute Engine instance.

00:30:19.620 --> 00:30:21.805
And then from there you
do whatever you need.

00:30:21.805 --> 00:30:23.930
MARK MANDEL: I'm just
actually genuinely blown away

00:30:23.930 --> 00:30:26.455
that App Engine
can receive email.

00:30:26.455 --> 00:30:28.919
I didn't realize it could
do this, and it's so cool.

00:30:28.919 --> 00:30:31.460
FRANCESC CAMPOY: The best part
is that it has been there for,

00:30:31.460 --> 00:30:32.870
like, ever.

00:30:32.870 --> 00:30:34.840
It is really not new.

00:30:34.840 --> 00:30:37.990
It is very simple.

00:30:37.990 --> 00:30:41.372
And if you don't
know, it's there.

00:30:41.372 --> 00:30:42.650
It is good.

00:30:42.650 --> 00:30:44.574
MARK MANDEL: My mind
is literally blown.

00:30:44.574 --> 00:30:45.490
It is literally blown.

00:30:45.490 --> 00:30:48.990
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, it
is a pretty fun thing to do.

00:30:48.990 --> 00:30:50.840
And talking about
App Engine, we also

00:30:50.840 --> 00:30:53.630
have a second question of the
week coming from our friend,

00:30:53.630 --> 00:30:55.670
Stu Swartz, or "Schwartz."

00:30:55.670 --> 00:30:57.490
Tell us how you pronounce it.

00:30:57.490 --> 00:30:59.900
So it is about App
Engine services,

00:30:59.900 --> 00:31:02.057
which for the people
that have been using

00:31:02.057 --> 00:31:03.640
App Engine for a
while, you might have

00:31:03.640 --> 00:31:06.910
known as App Engine modules.

00:31:06.910 --> 00:31:09.450
And he's asking OK,
so when should I

00:31:09.450 --> 00:31:12.256
use App Engine versus GKE?

00:31:12.256 --> 00:31:15.255
And is this a scale
issue, and approach issue,

00:31:15.255 --> 00:31:16.130
implementation issue?

00:31:16.130 --> 00:31:17.588
And then the second
question, which

00:31:17.588 --> 00:31:20.220
is more related to App
Engine services is, should I

00:31:20.220 --> 00:31:22.280
have a bunch of services?

00:31:22.280 --> 00:31:24.750
Should I have a
bunch of projects

00:31:24.750 --> 00:31:27.390
and one service for a project?

00:31:27.390 --> 00:31:30.530
The answer to the second
part of the question

00:31:30.530 --> 00:31:33.720
is, well, have as many
services as you need.

00:31:33.720 --> 00:31:35.160
Microservices are awesome.

00:31:35.160 --> 00:31:35.970
MARK MANDEL: And when
you say services,

00:31:35.970 --> 00:31:36.750
we're talking about modules.

00:31:36.750 --> 00:31:37.390
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Modules, yeah.

00:31:37.390 --> 00:31:39.980
So you're going to have
basically a different app--

00:31:39.980 --> 00:31:42.490
it's basically a different
App Engine application,

00:31:42.490 --> 00:31:44.710
but they share the
same resources, right?

00:31:44.710 --> 00:31:48.490
So if you want to make them
communicate through, like--

00:31:48.490 --> 00:31:49.240
MARK MANDEL: REST.

00:31:49.240 --> 00:31:50.820
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
--REST, you can.

00:31:50.820 --> 00:31:54.379
But also you can use task
queues and all of these things.

00:31:54.379 --> 00:31:56.420
And it's very easy to
communicate because they're

00:31:56.420 --> 00:31:59.010
all in the same project.

00:31:59.010 --> 00:32:02.260
When to use more than
one project-- normally,

00:32:02.260 --> 00:32:04.830
I could reserve that to
different environments.

00:32:04.830 --> 00:32:06.980
So you have like prod,
QA, stuff like that.

00:32:06.980 --> 00:32:09.020
Then those are good
because that way

00:32:09.020 --> 00:32:11.380
you're not sharing the data
store, which is probably

00:32:11.380 --> 00:32:12.900
a good idea.

00:32:12.900 --> 00:32:16.484
You should not give access
to the prod data just

00:32:16.484 --> 00:32:17.400
to do the test, right?

00:32:17.400 --> 00:32:19.880
So it is a good
place to separate.

00:32:19.880 --> 00:32:23.800
But you should have all
your production services

00:32:23.800 --> 00:32:26.340
in one single project,
much easier to handle.

00:32:26.340 --> 00:32:28.610
And then about
the question, when

00:32:28.610 --> 00:32:31.282
to use App Engine
versus GKE, maybe you

00:32:31.282 --> 00:32:33.240
can give us a little bit
of background on that.

00:32:33.240 --> 00:32:33.630
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:32:33.630 --> 00:32:35.338
So I had this as an
interesting question.

00:32:35.338 --> 00:32:37.520
I'll hearken back for a
more detailed response

00:32:37.520 --> 00:32:41.114
to our episode number two,
compute as a continuum,

00:32:41.114 --> 00:32:43.030
where we go into much
nuanced detail about it.

00:32:43.030 --> 00:32:45.410
But I feel like it's
really more a question

00:32:45.410 --> 00:32:48.662
of how much flexibility
and control do you want?

00:32:48.662 --> 00:32:50.852
App Engine standard
and App Engine flexible

00:32:50.852 --> 00:32:52.560
will give you slightly
different versions

00:32:52.560 --> 00:32:55.200
of that, whereas App
Engine standard, platform

00:32:55.200 --> 00:32:57.102
as a service, we
dictate a lot of things

00:32:57.102 --> 00:32:58.060
about how you do stuff.

00:32:58.060 --> 00:33:00.510
But that gives you great scale,
which is really powerful.

00:33:00.510 --> 00:33:02.910
Flex, we give you a few
more configuration options,

00:33:02.910 --> 00:33:04.260
a few more bits and pieces.

00:33:04.260 --> 00:33:06.460
So you can run pretty much
anything you want on it.

00:33:06.460 --> 00:33:07.960
But now you can't scale to zero.

00:33:07.960 --> 00:33:09.400
We don't spin up quite as fast.

00:33:09.400 --> 00:33:11.500
So there's the tradeoff.

00:33:11.500 --> 00:33:14.660
But if he want still
the sort of capability

00:33:14.660 --> 00:33:16.640
such that things stay
up, if they fall over,

00:33:16.640 --> 00:33:19.185
they come back, you
still want to be

00:33:19.185 --> 00:33:20.830
able to deploy with
a rolling update,

00:33:20.830 --> 00:33:23.270
things like that, GKE is
a great place for that.

00:33:23.270 --> 00:33:24.682
But you now have
even more tweaks

00:33:24.682 --> 00:33:26.140
and knobs that you
can pull around.

00:33:26.140 --> 00:33:28.000
So you'll have a cluster
of machines in that case.

00:33:28.000 --> 00:33:29.416
You pay for that
cluster machines.

00:33:29.416 --> 00:33:31.696
You can auto scale
it to a degree.

00:33:31.696 --> 00:33:33.820
But then you can do things
like resource management

00:33:33.820 --> 00:33:38.290
or have jobs or now
schedule jobs as well once

00:33:38.290 --> 00:33:39.290
that comes out of alpha.

00:33:39.290 --> 00:33:41.430
But you have way more control.

00:33:41.430 --> 00:33:43.337
And you can start--
you can say, maybe

00:33:43.337 --> 00:33:45.670
I want to have stuff with a
whole bunch of memory in it,

00:33:45.670 --> 00:33:47.378
which is way more than
I'd be able to get

00:33:47.378 --> 00:33:49.671
on App Engine or something
like that, persistent volume

00:33:49.671 --> 00:33:50.170
amounts.

00:33:50.170 --> 00:33:52.290
If there's a state that I
want to go to put in places,

00:33:52.290 --> 00:33:53.665
things that you
can't necessarily

00:33:53.665 --> 00:33:54.920
do as easy on App Engine.

00:33:54.920 --> 00:33:57.290
So it's probably really down
to what sort of application

00:33:57.290 --> 00:33:59.699
you're building, and then
what sort of capabilities

00:33:59.699 --> 00:34:01.490
you really want to be
able to have at hand.

00:34:01.490 --> 00:34:02.406
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:34:02.406 --> 00:34:05.470
My rule of thumb is, if
it can run on App Engine,

00:34:05.470 --> 00:34:07.700
it will run on App Engine,
because that way I can

00:34:07.700 --> 00:34:09.989
forget about all of the devops.

00:34:09.989 --> 00:34:11.790
And I just deploy
it, and I know it

00:34:11.790 --> 00:34:13.929
will be running there forever.

00:34:13.929 --> 00:34:14.989
And that makes me happy.

00:34:14.989 --> 00:34:17.800
But if it's something that
I'm going to need to be like,

00:34:17.800 --> 00:34:19.720
oh no, I have all
of these services

00:34:19.720 --> 00:34:21.219
and there's
dependencies across them

00:34:21.219 --> 00:34:22.885
and I want them to
be able to scale them

00:34:22.885 --> 00:34:24.659
independently and scale
them independently

00:34:24.659 --> 00:34:27.939
and all that stuff, then GKE
is probably the best place.

00:34:27.939 --> 00:34:29.480
MARK MANDEL: Seems
pretty reasonable.

